 {"id":"OBS-OB-001","title":"[P1] Minimal viable Prometheus emission: /metrics/prom with 5 series via client_golang+promhttp","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":2,"lane":"monitoring","type":"task","created_by":"monitoring-lane","source":"obs-audit-2026-11-03","created_at":"2026-11-03T21:05:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T18:08:14Z","attempts":0,"capability_tags":["observability","prometheus","metrics-endpoint"],"reasoning":"WHY: the lab is currently unobservable by any collector. It HAS a metrics route (internal/api/server.go:118 mux.HandleFunc(\"GET /metrics\", s.handleMetrics)) but handleMetrics (server.go:313-320) serves writeJSON of a bespoke metrics.Snapshot (internal/metrics/metrics.go:219-252): no Prometheus client library exists anywhere in the repo (grep -rn 'prometheus|promhttp|client_golang' over *.go/*.mod = 0 hits), no /metrics text format, and no scrape job. Live proof of the cost: GET :8766/metrics returns skipped_host_pressure=89, solve_count=0, while /api/v1/stats returns queue_depth=20 - the loop has been gate-skipping every tick for 1h29m of uptime at load1=14.77 against -load-threshold 4 (deploy/off-by-one.service:62) and nothing alerts. WHY THESE 5 FIRST: they answer the only 3am question that matters - is it up, is it doing work, is work backing up. WHAT: add prometheus/client_golang + promhttp to go.mod; register a PRIVATE registry on api.Server (do NOT use the global default registry and do NOT register NewGoCollector - the fleet already drowns in ~2000 self/exporter families for ~20 real app series); serve promhttp.HandlerFor(reg) at a NEW path GET /metrics/prom (leave the existing JSON /metrics byte-identical - docs/dogfood/diagnostics.md:1075-1116 and docs/dogfood/2026-10-16-operator-integration.md:25,48,92 consume it). Emit: obo_http_up (gauge), obo_cron_loop_running (gauge, false when main.go:225 'cron loop not started' fires), obo_solves_total{outcome=succeeded|failed|rejected} (counter - wired from internal/cron/loop.go:101